# Statement and Argument and Inference: Judging Reasons and Conclusions

> Verbal reasoning moves from finding what a statement *assumes* to
> judging what it *argues* and what *follows*. A statement and
> argument question hands you a proposal — "should exams go
> online?" — plus four arguments, and asks which are strong. An
> inference question gives premises and asks what must be true.
> Both test the same muscle: separating good reasoning from
> persuasive-sounding noise.

[important]
The examiner's ground rules: judge the **argument's strength**, never
whether you personally agree. A strong argument is relevant, addresses
the *main* proposal directly, and is well-supported by fact or
established principle. Anything vague, extreme, half-off-topic, or
merely emotional is weak — however nicely it is worded.
[/important]

## 1. Strong vs Weak Arguments: The Filters

| Filter | Strong argument passes | Weak argument fails |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Relevance | Bears on the proposal itself | Attacks a side issue |
| Representativeness | Applies generally, not one stray case | "My cousin flunked online, so it's bad" |
| Evidence | Facts, experience, established theory | Bare assertion or prediction |
| Proportion | Scales with the issue | Trivial benefit vs major disruption |
| Tone | Reasoned | Emotional, parochial, absolutist ("always", "never") |

**Worked example.** Statement: *Should India ban all private
tuition?*

- Argument I: *No — tuition is the only support for students in
  overcrowded classrooms* (relevant, experiential — **strong**).
- Argument II: *Yes — tuition makes parents feel poor at parties*
  (status anxiety is trivial vs a ban's fallout — **weak**).
- Argument III: *Yes — a ban restores the classroom's monopoly on
  learning* (assumes what it must prove — **weak**, circular).
- Argument IV: *No — coaching is a Rs 50,000-crore industry
  employing lakhs; a sudden ban destroys livelihoods* (proportionate,
  factual — **strong**).

## 2. Three Classic Weak-Argument Species

[definition]
**Circular argument** — the conclusion sneaks into the premise
("private tuition should be banned because tutoring outside school
is wrong"). **Distant/diverted argument** — attacks something
adjacent (the cost of marker pens, in an online-exam debate).
**Absolutist argument** — claims too much ("ALL students hate
exams") and can be refuted by one counterexample.
[/definition]

Exams re-test these three endlessly because each *sounds* forceful.
The counter-move: read the argument asking "could this be true and
the proposal still be fine?" — if yes, the argument is weak.

## 3. Statement and Inference: What MUST Follow

Inference questions flip the power balance: you are no longer
weighing arguments but checking entailment. The standard options:

- **Definitely true** — the statement forces it (no outside
  knowledge needed).
- **Probably true** — statement supports but does not force it.
- **Definitely false** — statement rules it out.
- **Probably false** — statement leans against it.
- **Data inadequate** — the statement is silent.

**Worked example.** Statement: *Every applicant who cleared the
interview had either a postgraduate degree or three years of work
experience; Ravi cleared the interview.*

- *Ravi has a postgraduate degree* — not forced (experience was the
  alternative) — **data inadequate**, even though it *probably*
  tempts.
- *Ravi does not have both a degree and experience* — nothing rules
  out having both — **data inadequate**.
- *Ravi was interviewed* — forced by "cleared the interview" —
  **definitely true**.

[tip]
Inference answers travel only *downward* from general to specific.
"Every A has B or C" tells you about each A; it never tells you
which branch each A took. Draw the Venn in your head before reading
the options.
[/tip]

## 4. Syllogistic Inference Patterns

| Pattern | Valid form | Classic trap |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Modus ponens | All A are B; x is A — so x is B | Reading "All A are B" as "All B are A" (illicit conversion) |
| Modus tollens | All A are B; x is not B — so x is not A | Denying the antecedent: x not A, so x not B (INVALID) |
| Some-statements | Some A are B — gives only overlap | "Some A are B" + "Some B are C" — conclude NOTHING forced |
| Negations | No A are B — so No B are A (valid) | "No A are B" + "x is B" — so x is not A (valid; underused) |

**Worked example.** *All diabetics are patients; some patients are
children.* Conclusion options: (I) *Some diabetics are children* —
nothing forces the overlap to include diabetics — invalid. (II)
*All children are patients* — reverses a "some" — invalid. (III)
*Some patients are diabetics* — valid conversion — **follows**.

## 5. Statement and Conclusion vs Statement and Inference

The two question brands differ in strictness:

- **Statement and conclusion:** usually multiple statements and
  multiple conclusions; judge each independently, "follows / does not
  follow".
- **Statement and inference:** degree language (definitely /
  probably / inadequate); the answer set forces you to commit to a
  level of certainty, not just yes/no.

Both punish the same sin: importing your own knowledge. If the
statement never mentions cricket, the correct answer cannot depend
on cricket — no matter how true your cricket "fact" feels.

[example]
Statement: *The municipal corporation will fine any household whose
water connection is illegal, after a survey beginning next month.*

- *Some households have illegal connections* — implied by the
  scheme's design (why fine otherwise?), but is it *forced*? A law
  may be preventive — exams here accept "probably true", not
  "definitely true".
- *Households surveyed before next month face no fine* — the fine
  applies after the survey begins — "definitely true" in standard
  answer keys.
- *The corporation needs revenue* — motive speculation — data
  inadequate.

Reading the fine's trigger conditions — "after a survey beginning
next month" — does the work; every trap option plays on motive
instead of mechanics.
[/example]

## 6. Strategy Summary

1. **Bracket the proposal** — know exactly what is being argued
   before touching the options.
2. For arguments: run the five filters (relevance,
   representativeness, evidence, proportion, tone) — an argument
   failing two or more is weak.
3. For inferences: hunt the *forced* conclusion; when not forced,
   decide between "probably" and "data inadequate" by asking whether
   the statement even speaks to the claim.
4. **Never import outside facts** — the statement is a closed
   universe.
5. Eliminate in pairs: two options often mirror each other
   (definitely/probably true); the mirror tells you what the examiner
   considers the decision point.

[summary]
Statement-and-argument questions score reasoning quality: strong
arguments are relevant, representative, evidenced, proportionate,
and measured; weak ones are circular, diverted, absolutist, or
emotional. Statement-and-inference questions score entailment:
"definitely true" must be forced by the statement alone, "probably
true" is supported but not compelled, and "data inadequate" marks
silence — outside knowledge never enters. Syllogistic discipline
(valid conversion, modus tollens, the nothing-forcing power of
"some") plus option-pair elimination turns both formats into
mechanical checks. Judge the reasoning, not the opinion; extract
only what is forced, and note what the statement carefully never
says.
[/summary]
